## Frequently Asked Questions

## Can I File a Lemon Law Claim If I Leased My Vehicle?

Yes, California lemon law applies to both purchased and leased vehicles. If your leased car has a recurring defect that affects its use, safety, or value - and the manufacturer has failed to fix it after multiple attempts - you may be eligible for a refund or lease replacement.

## What If My Car’s Defect Only Happens Occasionally?

Intermittent defects can still qualify for a lemon law claim, but they can be harder to prove. Keep a detailed record of when the issue occurs, take videos if possible, and make sure to report it to the dealer as soon as you notice it.

## Can I File a Lemon Law Claim If My Car Was in an Accident?

It depends. If the defect existed before the accident and is unrelated to the damage caused by the crash, you may still have a valid claim. However, if the accident directly caused or worsened the issue, the manufacturer is unlikely to be held responsible. A lemon law attorney can review your case to determine your eligibility.

## Does Lemon Law Cover Motorcycles, RVs, or Boats?

Yes! If you purchased a new or used vehicle under warranty, including motorcycles, RVs, boats, or trailers, and it has persistent defects, you may be covered. CA lemon laws apply to most consumer vehicles used for personal or family purposes.

## Can I File a Lemon Law Claim If I Bought My Car Out of State?

Generally, CA lemon law only covers vehicles purchased or leased in California. However, if you bought your car in another state but registered and primarily used it in California, you could still have legal options. Each situation is different, so it’s important to discuss your case with an experienced attorney.
